::ALG2_U03_L03_Q01::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L03_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.RR.1; MLA.A2.RR.3
Question:
Solve sqrt(x - 4) \= 6.
{
=A. x \= 40#Correct. Squaring gives x - 4 \= 36, so x \= 40.
~B. x \= 10#This adds 4 to 6 rather than to 36.
~C. x \= 32#This subtracts 4 after squaring instead of adding it.
~D. x \= 2#This does not satisfy the original radical equation.
}


::ALG2_U03_L03_Q02::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L03_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.RR.1; MLA.A2.RR.3
Question:
Solve sqrt(2x + 3) \= 7.
{
~A. x \= 10#This does not account for squaring 7.
=B. x \= 23#Correct. Squaring gives 2x + 3 \= 49, so x \= 23.
~C. x \= 26#This forgets to divide by 2.
~D. x \= 46#This subtracts 3 but does not divide by 2.
}


::ALG2_U03_L03_Q03::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L03_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.RR.1; MLA.A2.RR.3
Question:
Solve sqrt(x + 1) \= x - 1.
{
~A. x \= 0#x \= 0 is extraneous because the right side is -1.
~B. x \= 0 and x \= 3#Both arise after squaring, but x \= 0 fails the original equation.
=C. x \= 3#Correct. Squaring gives x + 1 \= x² - 2x + 1, so x(x - 3) \= 0; only x \= 3 checks.
~D. no solution#x \= 3 satisfies the original equation.
}


::ALG2_U03_L03_Q04::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L03_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.RR.1; MLA.A2.RR.3
Question:
Solve sqrt(4x - 3) \= 5.
{
~A. x \= 5#This does not make the radicand 25.
~B. x \= 22#This misses division by 4.
~C. x \= 28#This is 4x, not x.
=D. x \= 7#Correct. Squaring gives 4x - 3 \= 25, so 4x \= 28 and x \= 7.
}


::ALG2_U03_L03_Q05::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L03_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.RR.1; MLA.A2.RR.3
Question:
Which solution checks for sqrt(x + 4) \= x - 2?
{
=A. x \= 5#Correct. Squaring gives x \= 0 or x \= 5, but x \= 0 makes the right side negative.
~B. x \= 0#x \= 0 is extraneous because 2 is not equal to -2.
~C. x \= 0 and x \= 5#Only x \= 5 checks in the original equation.
~D. no solution#x \= 5 satisfies the original equation.
}


::ALG2_U03_L03_Q06::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L03_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.RR.1; MLA.A2.RR.3
Question:
Solve cuberoot(x - 1) \= 4.
{
~A. x \= 5#This adds 1 to 4 instead of cubing 4.
=B. x \= 65#Correct. Cubing gives x - 1 \= 64, so x \= 65.
~C. x \= 63#This subtracts 1 after cubing instead of adding it.
~D. x \= 16#This uses 4 squared, not 4 cubed.
}


::ALG2_U03_L03_Q07::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L03_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.RR.1; MLA.A2.RR.3
Question:
Solve sqrt(x/2) \= 3.
{
~A. x \= 6#This multiplies 3 by 2 but does not square first.
~B. x \= 9#This is x/2, not x.
=C. x \= 18#Correct. Squaring gives x/2 \= 9, so x \= 18.
~D. x \= 12#This does not satisfy the original equation.
}


::ALG2_U03_L03_Q08::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L03_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.RR.1; MLA.A2.RR.3
Question:
A model h \= sqrt(64t) gives height-related output h. If h \= 16, find t.
{
~A. t \= 2#This divides 16 by 8 but does not square first.
~B. t \= 8#This does not solve 256 \= 64t.
~C. t \= 16#This is the output value, not t.
=D. t \= 4#Correct. 16 \= sqrt(64t), so 256 \= 64t and t \= 4.
}


::ALG2_U03_L03_Q09::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L03_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.RR.1; MLA.A2.RR.3
Question:
What is the first algebraic step for sqrt(x + 8) \= 9?
{
=A. square both sides#Correct. The radical is already isolated, so square both sides to undo the square root.
~B. subtract 8 from both sides#The 8 is inside the radical, so this is not the first step.
~C. divide by 9#There is no coefficient to divide by.
~D. cube both sides#A square root is undone by squaring.
}


::ALG2_U03_L03_Q10::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L03_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.RR.1; MLA.A2.RR.3
Question:
What makes a candidate solution extraneous in a radical equation?
{
~A. it is always outside every radical domain#Some candidates are in the domain but still fail the original equation.
=B. it does not satisfy the original equation#Correct. A candidate solution must make the original radical equation true.
~C. it is negative#Negative values are not automatically extraneous; the original equation decides.
~D. it makes the radicand zero#A radicand of zero can be valid if the whole original equation checks.
}


::ALG2_U03_L03_Q11::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L03_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.RR.1; MLA.A2.RR.3
Question:
Solve sqrt(x + 10) - 2 \= 4.
{
~A. x \= 6#This stops after isolating the square root.
~B. x \= 14#This squares 4 before isolating the radical.
=C. x \= 26#Correct. Add 2 to get sqrt(x + 10) \= 6, then square to get x + 10 \= 36.
~D. x \= 38#This adds 10 instead of subtracting it after squaring.
}


::ALG2_U03_L03_Q12::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L03_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.RR.1; MLA.A2.RR.3
Question:
Solve 2sqrt(x - 1) \= 10.
{
~A. x \= 6#This forgets to square 5.
~B. x \= 24#This subtracts 1 instead of adding it.
~C. x \= 101#This squares before dividing by 2.
=D. x \= 26#Correct. Divide by 2 to get sqrt(x - 1) \= 5, then square.
}


::ALG2_U03_L03_Q13::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L03_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.RR.1; MLA.A2.RR.3
Question:
Which equation results from squaring sqrt(3x - 2) \= x?
{
=A. 3x - 2 \= x²#Correct. Squaring both sides gives the radicand equal to x squared.
~B. 3x - 2 \= x#The right side must also be squared.
~C. 9x² - 4 \= x²#The radicand itself is not squared after removing the square root.
~D. 3x \= x²#This drops the -2 term.
}


::ALG2_U03_L03_Q14::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L03_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.RR.1; MLA.A2.RR.3
Question:
Solve sqrt(x + 12) \= x.
{
~A. x \= -3#x \= -3 fails because the right side is negative.
=B. x \= 4#Correct. Squaring gives x + 12 \= x², so x \= 4 or -3; only x \= 4 checks.
~C. x \= -3 and x \= 4#Only x \= 4 checks in the original equation.
~D. no solution#x \= 4 satisfies the original equation.
}


::ALG2_U03_L03_Q15::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L03_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.RR.1; MLA.A2.RR.3
Question:
A solution to a radical model is t \= -4 seconds. What should happen in context?
{
~A. keep it because all algebraic solutions work#Context restrictions must be checked.
~B. change it to t \= 4#Changing the sign changes the solution.
=C. reject it because time cannot be negative#Correct. Elapsed time is usually restricted to nonnegative values.
~D. use it as the maximum#A negative time is not automatically a maximum.
}


::ALG2_U03_L03_Q16::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L03_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.RR.1; MLA.A2.RR.3
Question:
Solve sqrt(5x) \= 10.
{
~A. x \= 2#This divides 10 by 5 before squaring.
~B. x \= 10#This does not make 5x equal 100.
~C. x \= 100#This is the squared output, not x.
=D. x \= 20#Correct. Squaring gives 5x \= 100, so x \= 20.
}


::ALG2_U03_L03_Q17::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L03_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.RR.1; MLA.A2.RR.3
Question:
Solve sqrt(x - 7) \= 0.
{
=A. x \= 7#Correct. The square root is 0 only when the radicand is 0.
~B. x \= 0#Substituting 0 gives a negative radicand.
~C. x \= -7#This does not make x - 7 equal 0.
~D. no solution#x \= 7 checks.
}


::ALG2_U03_L03_Q18::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L03_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.RR.1; MLA.A2.RR.3
Question:
Which candidate should be rejected for sqrt(x + 6) \= x?
{
~A. x \= 3#x \= 3 checks because sqrt(9) \= 3.
=B. x \= -2#Correct. x \= -2 can appear after squaring, but the right side is negative and does not equal the principal square root.
~C. both x \= -2 and x \= 3#x \= 3 is valid.
~D. neither candidate#x \= -2 fails the original equation.
}


::ALG2_U03_L03_Q19::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L03_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.RR.1; MLA.A2.RR.3
Question:
Solve cuberoot(2x + 1) \= 3.
{
~A. x \= 1#This does not cube 3.
~B. x \= 14#This forgets to divide by 2.
=C. x \= 13#Correct. Cubing gives 2x + 1 \= 27, so x \= 13.
~D. x \= 26#This is 2x, not x.
}


::ALG2_U03_L03_Q20::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L03_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.RR.1; MLA.A2.RR.3
Question:
What does an extraneous solution mean?
{
~A. a solution with a radical symbol#Valid radical solutions can include radical notation.
~B. a negative number always#Negative numbers are not automatically extraneous.
~C. a solution found by checking#Checking identifies whether it is valid or extraneous.
=D. a candidate that fails the original equation#Correct. It may solve a transformed equation but not the original.
}

